<!-- ═══ SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS & RECOMMENDATIONS ═══ -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>6. Key Findings & Policy Implications</h2>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>1. Brent Crude drives retail prices strongly — but pass-through is asymmetric.</strong>
    <p>Petrol correlates at r = {round(r_petrol, 3)} with Brent Crude across the full period. However, retail prices fall more slowly than crude during demand collapses (COVID-19: crude dropped {covid_brent_drop}% vs. retail petrol {covid_petrol_drop}%), due to the floor effect of fixed taxes and subsidies. Policymakers should recognize that tax structures create price rigidity on the downside while amplifying upside risk for unsubsidized consumers.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>2. High subsidies reduce consumer prices by {subsidy_dampening}% but weaken market signals.</strong>
    <p>High-subsidy countries average ${high_sub_petrol}/L for petrol vs. ${low_sub_petrol}/L in low-subsidy nations. While this shields consumers, it also weakens the price signal that would otherwise incentivize fuel efficiency and demand reduction. Subsidy design should include automatic phase-out mechanisms tied to income thresholds or crude price bands.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>3. The subsidy buffer is most visible during crises — but at high fiscal cost.</strong>
    <p>During the 2022 energy crisis, the petrol price gap between low-subsidy and high-subsidy countries reached ${round(crisis_spread, 3)}/L. This demonstrates the protective effect of subsidies during extreme events, but sustaining such gaps requires substantial public expenditure. Targeted, time-limited subsidies triggered by price thresholds offer better fiscal sustainability than blanket subsidies.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>4. Tax policy diverges sharply by income level, creating equity concerns.</strong>
    <p>High-income countries impose average fuel taxes of {income_tax.loc[income_tax['income_level'] == 'High', 'mean'].values[0]}% vs. {income_tax.loc[income_tax['income_level'] == 'Low', 'mean'].values[0]}% in low-income countries. Paradoxically, low-income consumers already paying lower pre-tax prices may still face a higher <em>affordability burden</em> relative to income. Tax calibration should account for purchasing power parity, not just absolute price levels.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>5. Regional pricing inequality remains persistent, with spreads widening during disruptions.</strong>
    <p>The regional price spread ranged from ${regional_spread['spread'].min()} to ${regional_spread['spread'].max()} USD/L across the study period. Crises amplify inequality: regions with high taxation and limited subsidies see disproportionate price increases. International coordination on strategic reserves and price stabilization mechanisms can narrow this gap.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>6. Volatility is a policy outcome, not just a market outcome.</strong>
    <p>Year-over-year volatility (CV) differs substantially across income groups and subsidy regimes, confirming that domestic policy choices — not just global crude markets — determine how much price risk consumers bear. Countries seeking price stability should consider stabilization funds that accumulate revenue during low-price periods and release it during spikes.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>
  <ol style="margin-left:20px; color: var(--text-secondary);">
    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Implement automatic stabilizer mechanisms:</strong> Design subsidy programs with built-in triggers that increase support when Brent Crude exceeds a defined threshold and phase out as prices normalize, reducing fiscal drag.</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Calibrate taxes to affordability:</strong> Shift from flat percentage-based fuel taxes toward structures that consider GNI per capita, ensuring that fuel costs remain a proportionate share of household expenditure across income levels.</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Establish regional price stabilization funds:</strong> Pool resources at the regional level (especially in Africa and South America, where spreads are widest) to buffer against extreme crude price shocks without requiring permanent subsidies.</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Protect market signals during non-crisis periods:</strong> Allow retail prices to reflect crude movements during normal market conditions, reserving subsidy intervention for crisis-level thresholds. This preserves demand-side efficiency incentives.</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Improve data transparency:</strong> The high week-to-week variability in tax percentages within countries suggests estimation uncertainty. Standardized, transparent tax reporting would improve policy analysis and public trust.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

