ies show a <span class="pos">{subsidy_dampening}% lower</span> average petrol price than low-subsidy countries. Critically, the Brent-to-petrol correlation also differs: high-subsidy countries exhibit a weaker correlation with crude prices, confirming that subsidies partially decouple retail prices from global commodity markets.</p>", "+  <p>The subsidy gradient is stark: Very High-subsidy countries pay an average of ${vh_petrol}/L for petrol — <span class="pos">{dampening_vh_vs_low}% less</span> than Low-subsidy countries at ${l_petrol}/L. Equally important, the first-difference Brent correlation declines with subsidy intensity, confirming that higher subsidies decouple short-term retail price movements from crude oil fluctuations.</p>", " ", "-  <h3>4.2 Subsidy Effect by Income Level</h3>", "+  <h3>4.2 Yearly Petrol Price by Subsidy Level</h3>", "+  <div class="chart med"><canvas id="subsidyTrendChart"></canvas></div>", "+  <p>The chart above reveals that all subsidy tiers track the same macro trend, but the amplitude of swings is dramatically compressed at higher subsidy levels. During the 2022 crisis, Low-subsidy countries bore the full force of the Brent spike, while Very High-subsidy nations saw minimal retail impact.</p>", "+", "+  <h3>4.3 Subsidy Buffering During the 2022 Energy Crisis</h3>", "   <table>", "-    <thead><tr><th>Income Level</th><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Diesel (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Tax %</th></tr></thead>", "+    <thead><tr><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Avg Petrol Price (2022)</th></tr></thead>", "     <tbody>", " """", " ", "-for _, row in subsidy_income.iterrows():", "-    html += f"""      <tr><td>{row['income_level']}</td><td><span class="tag {'tag-high' if row['subsidy_level'] == 'High' else 'tag-low'}">{row['subsidy_level']}</span></td><td>${row['petrol_usd_liter']}</td><td>${row['diesel_usd_liter']}</td><td>{row['tax_percentage']}%</td></tr>\n"""", "+for _, row in crisis_subsidy.iterrows():", "+    html += f"      <tr><td>{sub_tag(row['subsidy_level'])}</td><td>${row['petrol_usd_liter']}</td></tr>\n"", " ", "+crisis_l = crisis_subsidy.loc[crisis_subsidy['subsidy_level'] == 'Low', 'petrol_usd_liter'].values[0]", "+crisis_vh = crisis_subsidy.loc[crisis_subsidy['subsidy_level'] == 'Very High', 'petrol_usd_liter'].values[0]", "+crisis_gap = round(crisis_l - crisis_vh, 3)", "+", " html += f"""    </tbody>", "   </table>", "+  <p>During 2022, the petrol price gap between Low-subsidy and Very High-subsidy countries reached <span class="hl">${crisis_gap}/L</span> — demonstrating that subsidies provided a powerful buffer against extreme crude price spikes, though at significant fiscal cost to governments.</p>", " ", "-  <h3>4.3 Pass-Through: Brent Correlation by Subsidy & Income</h3>", "+  <h3>4.4 Pass-Through by Subsidy Level & Income</h3>", "   <table>", "-    <thead><tr><th>Income Level</th><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Brent–Petrol Correlation (r)</th></tr></thead>", "+    <thead><tr><th>Income Level</th><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Avg 1st-Diff Correlation (r)</th></tr></thead>", "     <tbody>", " """", " ", " for _, row in pass_through.iterrows():", "-    html += f"""      <tr><td>{row['income_level']}</td><td><span class="tag {'tag-high' if row['subsidy_level'] == 'High' else 'tag-low'}">{row['subsidy_level']}</span></td><td>{row['brent_petrol_corr']}</td></tr>\n"""", "+    fd_val = row['fd_corr_mean']", "+    fd_str = f"{fd_val:.3f}" if isinstance(fd_val, (int, float, np.floating)) and not np.isnan(fd_val) else "Insufficient data"", "+    html += f"      <tr><td>{row['income_level']}</td><td>{sub_tag(row['subsidy_level'])}</td><td>{fd_str}</td></tr>\n"", " ", " html += f"""    </tbody>", "   </table>", "-  <p>High-subsidy, low-income countries show the weakest Brent–retail correlation, indicating the greatest decoupling from global markets. In contrast, low-subsidy, high-income nations pass through crude price changes almost fully.</p>", "+  <p>The first-difference pass-through correlation is systematically lower for high-subsidy countries across all income levels, confirming that subsidies dampen not just the level of prices but also the speed and magnitude with which crude price changes reach consumers.</p>", " ", "-  <h3>4.4 Subsidy Buffering During the 2022 Crisis</h3>", "+  <h3>4.5 Subsidy Level Distribution by Income</h3>", "   <table>", "-    <thead><tr><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Avg Petrol Price (2022)</th></tr></thead>", "+    <thead><tr><th>Income Level</th><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Countries</th><th>Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Tax %</th></tr></thead>", "     <tbody>", " """", " ", "-for _, row in crisis_subsidy.iterrows():", "-    html += f"""      <tr><td><span class="tag {'tag-high' if row['subsidy_level'] == 'High' else 'tag-low'}">{row['subsidy_level']}</span></td><td>${row['petrol_usd_liter']}</td></tr>\n"""", "+for _, row in subsidy_income.iterrows():", "+    html += f"      <tr><td>{row['income_level']}</td><td>{sub_tag(row['subsidy_level'])}</td><td>{int(row['country'])}</td><td>${row['petrol_usd_liter']}</td><td>{row['tax_percentage']}%</td></tr>\n"", " ", "-crisis_spread = crisis_subsidy.loc[crisis_subsidy['subsidy_level'] == 'Low', 'petrol_usd_liter'].values[0] - crisis_subsidy.loc[crisis_subsidy['subsidy_level'] == 'High', 'petrol_usd_liter'].values[0]", "-", " html += f"""    </tbody>", "   </table>", "-  <p>During the 2022 crisis, the price gap between low-subsidy and high-subsidy countries widened to ${round(crisis_spread, 3)}/L, demonstrating that subsidies provided a measurable buffer against extreme crude price spikes — though at significant fiscal cost.</p>", "-", "-  <h3>4.5 Yearly Petrol Price by Subsidy Level</h3>", "-  <div class="chart-container short">", "-    <canvas id="subsidyTrendChart"></canvas>", "-  </div>", " </div>", " ", "-<!-- ═══ SECTION 5: INCOME LEVEL SEGMENTATION ═══ -->", "+<!-- ═══ 5. INCOME LEVEL SEGMENTATION ═══ -->", " <div class="section">", "   <h2>5. Fuel Affordability by Income Level</h2>", " "]}, {"oldStart": 668, "oldLines": 7, "newStart": 536, "newLines": 7, "lines": [" """", " ", " for _, row in income_avg.iterrows():", "-    html += f"""          <tr><td>{row['income_level']}</td><td>${row['petrol_usd_liter']}</td><td>${row['diesel_usd_liter']}</td><td>${row['lpg_usd_liter']}</td></tr>\n"""", "+    html += f"          <tr><td>{row['income_level']}</td><td>${row['petrol_usd_liter']}</td><td>${row['diesel_usd_liter']}</td><td>${row['lpg_usd_liter']}</td></tr>\n"", " ", " html += f"""        </tbody>", "       </table>"]}, {"oldStart": 680, "oldLines": 18, "newStart": 548, "newLines": 20, "lines": [" """", " ", " for _, row in income_tax.iterrows():", "-    html += f"""          <tr><td>{row['income_level']}</td><td>{row['mean']}%</td><td>{row['min']}%</td><td>{row['max']}%</td></tr>\n"""", "+    html += f"          <tr><td>{row['income_level']}</td><td>{row['mean']}%</td><td>{row['min']}%</td><td>{row['max']}%</td></tr>\n"", " ", "+high_tax = income_tax.loc[income_tax['income_level'] == 'High', 'mean'].values[0]", "+mid_tax = income_tax.loc[income_tax['income_level'] == 'Middle', 'mean'].values[0]", "+low_tax = income_tax.loc[income_tax['income_level'] == 'Low', 'mean'].values[0]", "+", " html += f"""        </tbody>", "       </table>", "     </div>", "   </div>", " ", "   <h3>5.2 Tax-to-Price Ratio by Income Level</h3>", "-  <div class="chart-container short">", "-    <canvas id="taxIncomeChart"></canvas>", "-  </div>", "-  <p>High-income countries impose substantially higher average tax rates on fuel ({income_tax.loc[income_tax['income_level'] == 'High', 'mean'].values[0]}%), compared to middle-income ({income_tax.loc[income_tax['income_level'] == 'Middle', 'mean'].values[0]}%) and low-income ({income_tax.loc[income_tax['income_level'] == 'Low', 'mean'].values[0]}%) nations. This reflects both fiscal capacity and policy choices around using fuel taxes for revenue and demand management.</p>", "+  <div class="chart short"><canvas id="taxIncomeChart"></canvas></div>", "+  <p>High-income countries impose substantially higher average fuel tax rates ({high_tax}%) compared to middle-income ({mid_tax}%) and low-income ({low_tax}%) nations. This reflects both fiscal capacity and deliberate policy choices around using fuel taxes for revenue generation and demand management.</p>", " ", "   <h3>5.3 Price Stability by Income Level</h3>", "   <table>"]}, {"oldStart": 700, "oldLines": 59, "newStart": 570, "newLines": 60, "lines": [" """", " ", " for _, row in income_stability.iterrows():", "-    interp = "Most stable" if row['petrol_cv'] == income_stability['petrol_cv'].min() else ("Least stable" if row['petrol_cv'] == income_stability['petrol_cv'].max() else "Moderate stability")", "-    html += f"""      <tr><td>{row['income_level']}</td><td>{row['petrol_cv']:.2%}</td><td>{row['diesel_cv']:.2%}</td><td>{interp}</td></tr>\n"""", "+    is_min = row['petrol_cv'] == income_stability['petrol_cv'].min()", "+    is_max = row['petrol_cv'] == income_stability['petrol_cv'].max()", "+    interp = "Most stable" if is_min else ("Least stable" if is_max else "Moderate")", "+    html += f"      <tr><td>{row['income_level']}</td><td>{row['petrol_cv']:.2%}</td><td>{row['diesel_cv']:.2%}</td><td>{interp}</td></tr>\n"", " ", " html += f"""    </tbody>", "   </table>", "+  <p>Price stability (inverse of CV) varies across income groups, reflecting differences in the mix of subsidy and tax regimes within each category.</p>", " ", "   <h3>5.4 Yearly Petrol Price Trends by Income Level</h3>", "-  <div class="chart-container short">", "-    <canvas id="incomeTrendChart"></canvas>", "-  </div>", "+  <div class="chart med"><canvas id="incomeTrendChart"></canvas></div>", " </div>", " ", "-<!-- ═══ SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS & RECOMMENDATIONS ═══ -->", "+<!-- ═══ 6. KEY FINDINGS & RECOMMENDATIONS ═══ -->", " <div class="section">", "   <h2>6. Key Findings & Policy Implications</h2>", " ", "   <div class="finding">", "-    <strong>1. Brent Crude drives retail prices strongly — but pass-through is asymmetric.</strong>", "-    <p>Petrol correlates at r = {round(r_petrol, 3)} with Brent Crude across the full period. However, retail prices fall more slowly than crude during demand collapses (COVID-19: crude dropped {covid_brent_drop}% vs. retail petrol {covid_petrol_drop}%), due to the floor effect of fixed taxes and subsidies. Policymakers should recognize that tax structures create price rigidity on the downside while amplifying upside risk for unsubsidized consumers.</p>", "+    <strong>1. Brent Crude is the dominant long-run driver of retail prices, but short-term pass-through is highly heterogeneous.</strong>", "+    <p>Global-average weekly petrol prices correlate with Brent at r = {round(r_weekly_petrol, 3)}, and week-over-week changes show r = {round(r_fd_petrol, 3)}. However, at the individual country level, the mean first-difference correlation is only r = {mean_country_fd_corr} — meaning domestic policy choices create substantial variation in how quickly crude price changes reach consumers. Policymakers should treat the global-average correlation as an upper bound; actual pass-through depends on national fiscal architecture.</p>", "   </div>", " ", "   <div class="finding">", "-    <strong>2. High subsidies reduce consumer prices by {subsidy_dampening}% but weaken market signals.</strong>", "-    <p>High-subsidy countries average ${high_sub_petrol}/L for petrol vs. ${low_sub_petrol}/L in low-subsidy nations. While this shields consumers, it also weakens the price signal that would otherwise incentivize fuel efficiency and demand reduction. Subsidy design should include automatic phase-out mechanisms tied to income thresholds or crude price bands.</p>", "+    <strong>2. Subsidies dramatically reduce consumer prices — by up to {dampening_vh_vs_low}% — and dampen volatility.</strong>", "+    <p>Very High-subsidy countries average ${vh_petrol}/L vs. ${l_petrol}/L in Low-subsidy nations. The first-difference Brent correlation declines systematically from Low to Very High subsidy levels, confirming that subsidies decouple retail prices from crude volatility. However, subsidies also weaken the price signals that incentivize fuel efficiency, creating a policy trade-off between consumer protection and demand-side efficiency.</p>", "   </div>", " ", "   <div class="finding">", "     <strong>3. The subsidy buffer is most visible during crises — but at high fiscal cost.</strong>", "-    <p>During the 2022 energy crisis, the petrol price gap between low-subsidy and high-subsidy countries reached ${round(crisis_spread, 3)}/L. This demonstrates the protective effect of subsidies during extreme events, but sustaining such gaps requires substantial public expenditure. Targeted, time-limited subsidies triggered by price thresholds offer better fiscal sustainability than blanket subsidies.</p>", "+    <p>During the 2022 energy crisis, the petrol price gap between Low-subsidy and Very High-subsidy countries reached ${crisis_gap}/L. This demonstrates the protective effect of subsidies during extreme events, but sustaining such gaps requires substantial public expenditure. Targeted, time-limited subsidies triggered by price thresholds offer better fiscal sustainability than blanket subsidies.</p>", "   </div>", " ", "   <div class="finding">", "-    <strong>4. Tax policy diverges sharply by income level, creating equity concerns.</strong>", "-    <p>High-income countries impose average fuel taxes of {income_tax.loc[income_tax['income_level'] == 'High', 'mean'].values[0]}% vs. {income_tax.loc[income_tax['income_level'] == 'Low', 'mean'].values[0]}% in low-income countries. Paradoxically, low-income consumers already paying lower pre-tax prices may still face a higher <em>affordability burden</em> relative to income. Tax calibration should account for purchasing power parity, not just absolute price levels.</p>", "+    <strong>4. Retail prices are asymmetrically rigid: they fall more slowly than crude during demand collapses.</strong>", "+    <p>During COVID-19, Brent Crude dropped {covid_brent_drop}% but retail petrol fell only {covid_petrol_drop}%. Fixed tax components and subsidy floor effects create downward price rigidity, meaning consumers benefit less from crude price declines than they suffer from increases. This asymmetry particularly disadvantages low-income consumers who spend a larger share of income on fuel.</p>", "   </div>", " ", "   <div class="finding">", "-    <strong>5. Regional pricing inequality remains persistent, with spreads widening during disruptions.</strong>", "-    <p>The regional price spread ranged from ${regional_spread['spread'].min()} to ${regional_spread['spread'].max()} USD/L across the study period. Crises amplify inequality: regions with high taxation and limited subsidies see disproportionate price increases. International coordination on strategic reserves and price stabilization mechanisms can narrow this gap.</p>", "+    <strong>5. Tax policy diverges sharply by income level, creating equity concerns.</strong>", "+    <p>High-income countries impose average fuel taxes of {high_tax}% vs. {low_tax}% in low-income countries. While low-income consumers pay lower absolute prices (partly due to subsidies), they may still face a higher affordability burden relative to income. Tax calibration should account for purchasing power parity, not just absolute price levels.</p>", "   </div>", " ", "   <div class="finding">", "-    <strong>6. Volatility is a policy outcome, not just a market outcome.</strong>", "-    <p>Year-over-year volatility (CV) differs substantially across income groups and subsidy regimes, confirming that domestic policy choices — not just global crude markets — determine how much price risk consumers bear. Countries seeking price stability should consider stabilization funds that accumulate revenue during low-price periods and release it during spikes.</p>", "+    <strong>6. Regional pricing inequality persists and widens during disruptions.</strong>", "+    <p>The regional price spread ranged from ${regional_spread['spread'].min()} to ${regional_spread['spread'].max()} USD/L. Crises amplify inequality as high-tax, low-subsidy regions see disproportionate increases. International coordination on strategic reserves and regional stabilization mechanisms can narrow this gap.</p>", "   </div>", " ", "   <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>", "-  <ol style="margin-left:20px; color: var(--text-secondary);">", "+  <ol style="margin-left:20px; color: var(--text-sec);">", "     <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Implement automatic stabilizer mechanisms:</strong> Design subsidy programs with built-in triggers that increase support when Brent Crude exceeds a defined threshold and phase out as prices normalize, reducing fiscal drag.</li>", "-    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Calibrate taxes to affordability:</strong> Shift from flat percentage-based fuel taxes toward structures that consider GNI per capita, ensuring that fuel costs remain a proportionate share of household expenditure across income levels.</li>", "-    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Establish regional price stabilization funds:</strong> Pool resources at the regional level (especially in Africa and South America, where spreads are widest) to buffer against extreme crude price shocks without requiring permanent subsidies.</li>", "-    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Protect market signals during non-crisis periods:</strong> Allow retail prices to reflect crude movements during normal market conditions, reserving subsidy intervention for crisis-level thresholds. This preserves demand-side efficiency incentives.</li>", "-    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Improve data transparency:</strong> The high week-to-week variability in tax percentages within countries suggests estimation uncertainty. Standardized, transparent tax reporting would improve policy analysis and public trust.</li>", "+    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Calibrate taxes to affordability:</strong> Shift from flat percentage-based fuel taxes toward structures that consider GNI per capita, ensuring fuel costs remain a proportionate share of household expenditure across income levels.</li>", "+    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Establish regional price stabilization funds:</strong> Pool resources at the regional level (especially in regions with the widest spreads) to buffer against extreme crude price shocks without requiring permanent subsidies.</li>", "+    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Protect market signals during non-crisis periods:</strong> Allow retail prices to reflect crude movements during normal conditions, reserving subsidy intervention for crisis-level thresholds. This preserves demand-side efficiency incentives.</li>", "+    <li style="margin-bottom:10px;"><strong>Address downward price rigidity:</strong> When crude prices fall, ensure tax and subsidy structures allow retail prices to follow — not just on the upside. Consider tax rebates or temporary subsidy reductions when Brent falls below a floor, passing savings to consumers.</li>", "   </ol>", " </div>", " "]}, {"oldStart": 765, "oldLines": 49, "newStart": 636, "newLines": 33, "lines": [" ", " <!-- ═══ CHARTS ═══ -->", " <script>", "-const COLORS = {{", "-  petrol: '#0066cc',", "-  diesel: '#e65100',", "-  lpg: '#2e7d32',", "-  brent: '#6a1b9a',", "-  highSub: '#c62828',", "-  lowSub: '#1565c0',", "-  highInc: '#00695c',", "-  midInc: '#f57f17',", "-  lowInc: '#ad1457',", "+const C = {{", "+  petrol: '#0066cc', diesel: '#e65100', lpg: '#2e7d32', brent: '#6a1b9a',", "+  veryHigh: '#880e4f', high: '#c62828', medium: '#e65100', low: '#1565c0',", "+  highInc: '#00695c', midInc: '#f57f17', lowInc: '#ad1457',", " }};", " ", "-const chartDefaults = {{", "-  responsive: true,", "-  maintainAspectRatio: false,", "-  plugins: {{", "-    legend: {{ position: 'top', labels: {{ usePointS